INSERT INTO dbo.KbDoc (DocName, Content) VALUES (N'availability-group-failover-runbook.md', N'# Runbook: Always On Availability Group Failover

## Purpose
Plan and execute failover of our production Always On Availability Group (AG), and
recognize when an automatic failover has happened.

## Topology
- AG name `AG-PROD`, two synchronous replicas in the primary datacenter (automatic
  failover between them) plus one asynchronous replica in the DR site (manual
  failover only, possible data loss).
- The listener `agprod-listener` is what applications connect to; it always points
  at the current primary. Apps should connect through the listener, never to a node.

## Planned failover (synchronous replica, no data loss)
1. Confirm the target secondary is **synchronized** (not just synchronizing) in
   the dashboard or `sys.dm_hadr_availability_replica_states`.
2. From the current primary, run `ALTER AVAILABILITY GROUP [AG-PROD] FAILOVER`
   while connected to the target secondary, or use the SSMS failover wizard.
3. Confirm the listener now resolves to the new primary and the app reconnects.

## Forced failover to DR (asynchronous, possible data loss)
- Only during a real datacenter outage and with incident-commander approval.
- `ALTER AVAILABILITY GROUP [AG-PROD] FORCE_FAILOVER_ALLOW_DATA_LOSS`. Because the
  DR replica is asynchronous, transactions not yet shipped are lost; record the
  estimated data loss from `sys.dm_hadr_database_replica_states`.

## After any failover
- Resume data movement on the old primary once it rejoins as a secondary.
- Run `DBCC CHECKDB` if the failover was due to suspected corruption.
- A database in an AG can''t be restored over directly — remove it from the AG
  first (see the backup/restore runbook).

## Recognizing an automatic failover
Symptoms: a brief connection blip, the listener pointing at a different node, and
AG health events in the `system_health` session and the SQL error log. Find out
*why* it failed over (node reboot, storage, quorum loss) before failing back.
');
INSERT INTO dbo.KbDoc (DocName, Content) VALUES (N'backup-restore-runbook.md', N'# Runbook: Backup and Restore (Production SQL Server)

## Purpose
How our team backs up production databases and how to restore them during an
incident. Owner: Database Platform team. Review cadence: quarterly.

## Backup schedule
- **Full backups:** nightly at 01:00 to the `\\backupsan\sql\full` share.
- **Differential backups:** every 6 hours.
- **Transaction log backups:** every 15 minutes for all databases in FULL
  recovery model. This caps potential data loss (RPO) at roughly 15 minutes.
- Backups are written with `CHECKSUM` and verified nightly with `RESTORE
  VERIFYONLY`. A weekly test restore runs on the recovery server.

## Restore: point-in-time recovery
1. Identify the most recent full backup before the target time, plus the
   differential and the chain of log backups up to the target time.
2. Restore the full backup `WITH NORECOVERY`.
3. Restore the latest differential `WITH NORECOVERY`.
4. Restore each log backup in order `WITH NORECOVERY`; for the final one use
   `WITH STOPAT = ''<target time>'', RECOVERY`.
5. Run `DBCC CHECKDB` on the restored database before handing it back to the app
   team.

## Common pitfalls
- A broken log chain (someone took an out-of-band backup, or the model was
  switched to SIMPLE) makes point-in-time restore impossible — you can only
  restore to the last full/diff. Never switch recovery models without a change
  ticket.
- Restoring over a database in an Availability Group requires removing it from
  the AG first. See the Availability Group failover runbook.
- Watch free space on the restore target; a restore needs room for the full data
  and log files.

## Escalation
If the most recent backups are missing or corrupt, page the on-call DBA and open
a Sev-2. Do not delete the existing (possibly damaged) database until a restore
is confirmed good.
');
INSERT INTO dbo.KbDoc (DocName, Content) VALUES (N'blocking-and-deadlocks-runbook.md', N'# Runbook: Blocking and Deadlocks

## Purpose
Diagnose and resolve blocking chains and deadlocks. Symptom from users is usually
"the application is frozen" or "saving takes forever."

## Triage: is it blocking?
1. Check `sys.dm_exec_requests` for sessions with a non-zero `blocking_session_id`.
2. Find the head blocker — the session that is blocking others but is not itself
   blocked. Look at what it''s waiting on (`wait_type`) and its open transaction
   (`sys.dm_tran_active_transactions`).
3. Common causes: a long-running transaction left open by an application bug, a
   report running under the default isolation level taking shared locks, or an
   index rebuild holding a schema lock.

## Resolving blocking
- Prefer fixing the head blocker (commit/rollback its transaction) over killing
  sessions. If you must, `KILL <session_id>` the head blocker and confirm the
  chain clears.
- Long-term fixes: add the missing index causing a scan-and-lock, enable Read
  Committed Snapshot Isolation (RCSI) so readers don''t block writers, or fix the
  app to keep transactions short.

## Deadlocks
- A deadlock is two sessions each holding a lock the other needs; SQL Server picks
  a victim and rolls it back (error 1205). The app should retry the victim.
- Capture the deadlock graph from the `system_health` Extended Events session
  (it''s on by default) to see the resources and queries involved.
- Typical fixes: access objects in a consistent order across transactions, keep
  transactions short, add a covering index to reduce lock footprint.

## Escalation
If blocking persists and is customer-impacting, page on-call. Note the head
blocker''s login and query so we can follow up with the owning team.
');
INSERT INTO dbo.KbDoc (DocName, Content) VALUES (N'failed-nightly-etl-postmortem.md', N'# Post-mortem: Nightly ETL Load Failure (2026-02-18)

## Summary
On the night of 2026-02-18 the nightly ETL load into the warehouse failed and the
morning dashboards were stale. Root cause was a transaction log that filled up
because an index rebuild ran at the same time as the bulk load, and the log backup
job could not keep up. Customer impact: ~3 hours of stale reporting. Severity: 2.

## Timeline
- 02:30 — the index maintenance job started a REBUILD of a large clustered index.
- 02:34 — the ETL bulk load began inserting ~40 million rows.
- 02:51 — the transaction log for the warehouse database hit 100% and the load
  failed with error 9002 ("transaction log is full").
- 03:05 — on-call paged. 03:20 — added a temporary log file to get breathing room
  and let the log backup drain the log. 05:10 — re-ran the ETL successfully.

## Root cause
The index rebuild and the bulk load both generated heavy transaction log at once.
Under FULL recovery the log can only be truncated after a log backup, and the
15-minute log backups couldn''t drain it fast enough. The two heavy jobs were never
meant to overlap; a schedule change three weeks earlier moved the ETL 30 minutes
earlier, into the maintenance window.

## What fixed it
- Added a second log file temporarily (error 9002 immediate relief).
- Took an out-of-cycle log backup to truncate the log.
- Re-ran the ETL after the rebuild finished.

## Preventive actions
- Separate the maintenance window from the ETL window; the index job now starts
  only after the ETL "load complete" signal.
- Pre-size the warehouse log for the known nightly volume instead of relying on
  autogrowth.
- Alert when log used % exceeds 70% during the load window.

## Lesson
Never schedule index rebuilds to overlap a large load on a FULL-recovery database.
See the index maintenance and backup/restore runbooks.
');
INSERT INTO dbo.KbDoc (DocName, Content) VALUES (N'index-maintenance-runbook.md', N'# Runbook: Index and Statistics Maintenance

## Purpose
Keep indexes and statistics healthy without causing production impact. Owner:
Database Platform team.

## Policy
- We use a threshold-based job (nightly, 02:30) rather than blanket rebuilds:
  - Fragmentation **5%–30%**: `ALTER INDEX ... REORGANIZE` (online, lightweight).
  - Fragmentation **> 30%**: `ALTER INDEX ... REBUILD`. Use `WITH (ONLINE = ON)`
    on Enterprise edition to avoid blocking; offline rebuilds take a schema-mod
    lock and will block the table.
  - Skip indexes smaller than 1,000 pages — fragmentation there is noise.
- Update statistics with `FULLSCAN` weekly on the largest tables; rely on
  auto-update for the rest.

## Symptoms that suggest maintenance is overdue
- Query plans suddenly choosing scans over seeks.
- Cardinality estimates wildly off (estimated vs actual rows differ by orders of
  magnitude) — usually stale statistics, fixed by an `UPDATE STATISTICS`.
- Growing logical fragmentation reported by `sys.dm_db_index_physical_stats`.

## Cautions
- Index **rebuilds generate a lot of transaction log**; on databases in FULL
  recovery this can balloon log backups. Schedule around the backup window.
- An online rebuild still needs a brief schema lock at the end to swap the index
  in; long-running transactions can block that final step.
- Do not rebuild during the nightly ETL load — see the failed-nightly-ETL
  post-mortem for what happens when these overlap.

## Vector indexes (SQL Server 2025)
DiskANN vector indexes are different from B-tree indexes and aren''t part of this
job. They''re rebuilt manually after large data changes; the table is read-only
while the (current) version of the vector index exists.
');
INSERT INTO dbo.KbDoc (DocName, Content) VALUES (N'tempdb-contention-runbook.md', N'# Runbook: tempdb Contention

## Purpose
Recognize and relieve tempdb allocation contention, which shows up as
intermittent, instance-wide slowness that doesn''t map to a single query.

## Symptoms
- Many sessions waiting on `PAGELATCH_UP` / `PAGELATCH_EX` on pages in database
  id 2 (tempdb), often pages like `2:1:1` (PFS), `2:1:2` (GAM), `2:1:3` (SGAM).
- Slowness correlates with workload that creates many temp tables or table
  variables, or heavy sort/hash spills.

## Diagnosis
1. Query `sys.dm_exec_requests` / `sys.dm_os_waiting_tasks` and look for
   `PAGELATCH` waits with a `resource_description` pointing at tempdb system pages.
2. Check how many tempdb data files exist and whether they''re equally sized.

## Standard fixes
- **Multiple, equally sized tempdb data files.** Start with a number equal to the
  number of logical processors up to 8; add more (in multiples of 4) only if
  contention persists. All files must be the **same size** with the **same
  autogrowth**, or the proportional-fill algorithm sends most allocations to one
  file and reintroduces the hot spot.
- Modern SQL Server enables uniform extent allocation and auto-growth-all-files
  for tempdb by default; confirm these are on.
- Reduce the workload''s tempdb footprint: fix spills with better indexes/stats,
  avoid unnecessary temp tables, and review code that creates large table
  variables.

## Cautions
- Do not put tempdb on the same slow volume as user data/log. Fast local NVMe is
  ideal; tempdb is recreated on restart so durability isn''t a concern.
- Changing tempdb file count/size requires a SQL Server service restart to take
  effect.
');
GO
